The '''Republic of Narubi''' is an island country in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]]. It comprises approximately 15 islands with a combined area of about 648 square kilometres and an estimated population of 184,520. Its capital and largest city is [[Naruba City]], on the island of [[Naruba]]. The country is primarily [[Burawi]]-speaking and is a member of the [[Adonian Community]].

The republic's islands were historically inhabited by the [[Narubi people|Narubi]] and [[Yamari people|Yamari]] peoples. They were trade contacts of [[Guajicutea]] rather than tributaries, and later came under [[Burawa|Burawi]] authority along with the rest of the eastern Capuyaquiran islands. Narubi was part of the [[Capuyaquiran League]] and, after 1832, the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]]. The province declared independence in 1971, following a movement in which its substantial share of the union's population and economic output sought self-government.

== Geography ==

The Republic of Narubi consists of roughly 15 islands in the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea. The largest island is [[Naruba]], which covers about 304 square kilometres and is the historic homeland of the [[Narubi people]]. Other islands include the [[Yamari people|Yamari]] islands. The republic's total land area is approximately 648 square kilometres, giving an average population density of about 278 inhabitants per square kilometre.

== History ==

=== Pre-colonial period ===

The islands of present-day Narubi were settled by the Narubi and Yamari peoples. Both groups maintained trade relationships with the neighbouring islands of the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea, including Guajicutea. Unlike several of the Murani and Dukuri islands, the Narubi and Yamari islands were trade contacts rather than Guaji tributaries, and retained their own local leadership and maritime traditions.

=== Burawi rule and the Capuyaquiran League ===

Following the expansion of Burawi authority across the Greater Capuyaquiran islands in the sixteenth century, the Narubi and Yamari islands came under Burawi control alongside the islands of the future United Capuyaquiran Republic. In 1755 they became part of the autonomous [[Capuyaquiran League]] under the Burawi protectorate. After 1832 they formed part of the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]], sharing the Burawi language and institutions of the union.

=== Secession (1971) ===

During the twentieth century, Narubi formed a substantial province of the United Capuyaquiran Republic, containing a large share of the union's economy and population concentrated around [[Naruba]]. A secessionist movement argued that the province's wealth and population justified separate statehood. The movement was strengthened by the aftermath of [[Hurricane Sahan]] in 1969: Narubians argued that reconstruction aid was distributed unevenly between the islands and that the province received less support than other parts of the union. In 1971, Narubi declared independence, and the [[Republic of Narubi]] was recognised as a separate state. The division left the remaining United Capuyaquiran Republic with its former capital, [[Maruba City]], and the islands of the eastern union.

== Economy ==

The Republic of Narubi has the highest gross domestic product per capita in the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea region, estimated at I$28,000. Its total GDP is approximately I$5.17 billion. The economy is centered on tourism, maritime services, and natural resources, supported by fishing and transport links to the wider Capuyaquiran region. The country has a [[Human Development Index]] of 0.790.

== Demographics ==

Narubi had an estimated population of 184,520 in 2025. The population is largely Burawi-speaking, reflecting the island's long integration into the Burawi sphere through the Capuyaquiran League and the United Capuyaquiran Republic. Narubian society preserves descent from the historic Narubi and Yamari peoples.

